{"data":{"actor_id":"01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771","created_at":"2026-10-04T22:25:52.978Z","event_type":"comment.status_changed","id":"282","payload":{"from":"open","reason":"REFUTED. Both stated premises are false for the code they name, and the stated\nconsequence is wrong in the opposite direction.\n\n1. \"stamps the header when Commit and Tree are both empty, but does not verify\n   that localCommit and localTree are valid\" -- empty is already refused.\n   semantic.go:1465 (at the reviewed commit ecc832c8):\n\n       if strings.TrimSpace(commit) == \"\" || strings.TrimSpace(tree) == \"\" {\n           return \"\"\n       }\n\n   which is a guard the test suite already holds. partial_clone_index_test.go:56\n   and :59 assert exactly this, including the whitespace-only case:\n\n       if note := stampSemanticHeaderFromLocalGit(\u0026empty, \"\", \"\"); note != \"\" {\n           t.Errorf(\"no local commit means no stamp, got %q\", note)\n       }\n       if note := stampSemanticHeaderFromLocalGit(\u0026empty, commit, \"  \"); note != \"\" {\n           t.Errorf(\"a blank tree means no stamp, got %q\", note)\n       }\n\n2. \"(e.g., from a failed git rev-parse)\" -- a failed rev-parse cannot reach the\n   stamp. gitScalar (semantic.go:2901) returns (\"\", err) on failure, and\n   runSemanticIndex returns at semantic.go:572-579 --\n\n       head, err := gitScalar(ctx, opts.Runner, repoDir, \"rev-parse\", \"HEAD\")\n       if err != nil {\n           return nameDegenerateRepoFailure(...)\n       }\n\n   -- which is BEFORE indexOpts.localCommit = head at semantic.go:587. There is\n   no path on which a failed rev-parse supplies the stamp value.\n\n3. \"which later validation at internal/cli/semantic.go:746 would then reject\"\n   -- it would not. Line 746 is\n\n       validateLiveSemanticHeader(header, head, tree, indexOpts.worktree \u0026\u0026 dirty)\n\n   and that function (semantic.go:1550-1555) compares header.Commit against\n   `commit` and header.Tree against `tree`. On the stamped path header.Commit\n   IS head and header.Tree IS tree, because the stamp just assigned them, so\n   both comparisons are a value against itself. The prescribed remedy was\n   justified by a downstream rejection that cannot occur.\n\nWhat is left of the finding is \"git rev-parse succeeded and printed something\nthat is not an object ID\". git does not do that: `rev-parse HEAD` either prints\na 40-hex OID or exits nonzero (unborn HEAD, corrupt repo), and case 2 covers the\nnonzero branch. Only a CommandRunner that lies -- i.e. a test stub -- can produce\nit, and a shape check on a test stub's output guards nothing in production.\n\nNote: the SEPARATE and real defect on this function, that nothing verified the\ncommit across the index run, is finding 01M44D0RFH560E384NKJVE2DCE and is fixed\nin 253681bf. That one was confirmed; this one is not the same defect.","to":"dismissed"},"review_id":"01M44CC8BB71XSZPF0F9JY7FRC","target_id":"01M44CC8BM26QXJ9XDGRM1M63W","target_type":"review_comment","trail_id":"01M3ZYBZG8PHYV8XPY98XXFB43"},"event":"comment.status_changed"}
