{"data":{"actor_id":"01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771","created_at":"2026-10-04T14:41:05.792Z","event_type":"comment.status_changed","id":"171","payload":{"from":"open","reason":"REFUTED by measurement. The premise is a misreading of fstat semantics.\n\n\"It stats the current inode, which could have changed if the FD was opened on a path that got replaced\" — a descriptor refers to one open file description, which refers to one inode. A rename gives the PATH a new inode; the descriptor keeps the old one. The inode a descriptor points at cannot change.\n\nMeasured on this machine, replacing the path by rename BETWEEN Open() and Stat():\n\n  FD   Stat(): size=11 mtime=08:40:43 inode=589326287\n  FD   read  : \"VERSION-ONE\"\n  PATH Stat(): size=21 mtime=10:40:43 inode=589326288\n  PATH read  : \"VERSION-TWO-DIFFERENT\"\n\nThe descriptor's metadata and its bytes describe the SAME version, which is exactly what the comment claims and exactly why the single handle was introduced. The old code took os.ReadFile then os.Stat on the PATH, which is the pairing that could straddle two versions; that is the bug this fixed.\n\nOn the suggested remedy: os.File.Stat() IS fstat(2) on the descriptor — that is what was measured above. There is no separate Fstat to call, and reordering changes nothing, because neither operation consults the path.\n\nOne caveat stated for the record, since it is the only way the concern could be made true: this holds because git replaces the index by RENAME. A writer that truncated and rewrote in place would mutate the inode the descriptor holds. No consistent snapshot is available against such a writer by any method short of taking the lock, which is the contention runGitWithScratchIndex exists to avoid, and the function's comment already says the snapshot can be overtaken by a later write.","to":"dismissed"},"review_id":"01M43NFVR54RQF2BJAZY7ZPWGQ","target_id":"01M43NFVRA7R2N3GSMQVM9W5R1","target_type":"review_comment","trail_id":"01M3ZYBZG8PHYV8XPY98XXFB43"},"event":"comment.status_changed"}
